WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.757
Qdrant moves us closer to a full vector database.

00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:08.937
A Qdrant collection stores vectors together with payload metadata.

00:00:08.937 --> 00:00:14.712
We specify the vector dimension and the distance metric, then insert points.

00:00:14.712 --> 00:00:19.482
Each point has an ID, a vector, and a payload.

00:00:19.482 --> 00:00:28.714
The payload can include fields such as title, topic, source, permission, or any other metadata useful for filtering.

00:00:29.245 --> 00:00:34.792
At query time, we embed the question and ask Qdrant for the nearest points.

00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:43.462
The classroom example can run in memory, but Qdrant can also run as a persistent server or managed cloud service.

00:00:43.462 --> 00:00:47.367
What should feel familiar is the information flow.

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:53.167
We still have chunk text, metadata, embeddings, and a query vector.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:59.497
The database simply gives us more infrastructure around those pieces, especially filtering, persistence,

00:00:59.497 --> 00:01:02.381
and service-style deployment.
